1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:13,807
Hurricanes. Twisters. Lightning. Knowing when they'll strike means life or death.

2
00:00:13,808 --> 00:00:15,000
Weather

3
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
prediction. Harnessing chaos. Now on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,060
A 20-foot-high surge of ocean water

5
00:00:41,061 --> 00:00:45,220
pounds stranded islanders as a hurricane devastates the Caribbean.

6
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:50,040
150-mile-per-hour winds blow through Puerto Rico,

7
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:51,920
turning lives upside down.

8
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:56,920
The path of destruction heads north through the Gulf of Mexico.

9
00:00:56,921 --> 00:01:08,660
By the time it crosses the Florida panhandle into Georgia, it's downgraded to a tropical depression, but still floods the state with two and a half feet of rain.

10
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,300
That's going to be one of the top stories in our newscast today, the downgraded hurricane.

11
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:22,660
On the other side of the country, Los Angeles weather forecaster Fritz Coleman monitors the storm from the newsroom of KNBC-TV.

12
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:32,580
So I'll ask the machine to pull me up Atlanta airport radar site.

13
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,640
Atlanta will occupy the middle of the screen here.

14
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:39,840
And now the machine, right about there, the machine will build the loop.

15
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:43,600
It'll do the last six or seven hours of frames of radar.

16
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:47,820
And we can watch this tropical depression move northeast across the eastern seaboard.

17
00:01:47,821 --> 00:01:54,820
Like thousands of other forecasters, Coleman can quickly access an amalgamation of data

18
00:01:54,821 --> 00:01:57,820
on weather conditions across the country.

19
00:01:57,821 --> 00:02:05,820
It is gathered from radar, satellites, and weather balloons.

20
00:02:05,821 --> 00:02:08,820
It comes from research vessels on the ocean

21
00:02:08,821 --> 00:02:14,820
and specially designed aircraft capable of flying through the eye of a hurricane.

22
00:02:14,821 --> 00:02:23,220
It is a complex weather prediction network that saves countless lives by alerting the public to the onset of disaster.

23
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:28,967
National Weather Service in Shreveport has issued a tornado warning effective until 3 p.m.

24
00:02:28,968 --> 00:02:30,220
Central Standard Time.

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,180
A key element amidst this array of technology is simple human observation.

26
00:02:38,181 --> 00:02:41,700
A tornado touchdown on the southeast side.

27
00:02:41,701 --> 00:02:46,280
looking up at the sky in an attempt to understand what occurs there.

28
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,620
It is the oldest form of weather prediction.

29
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:53,300
It likely began at the dawn of humanity.

30
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:03,240
Hunter-gatherers possibly living as long as 300,000 years ago

31
00:03:03,241 --> 00:03:07,240
had the ability to understand rudimentary weather relationships.

32
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:13,720
Clearly with the hunter gathering abode of prehistorical people, weather had to be even

33
00:03:13,721 --> 00:03:17,080
more dominant in their lives than it is right now.

34
00:03:17,081 --> 00:03:22,900
So it stands to reason that they must have watched it and observed it very carefully.

35
00:03:22,901 --> 00:03:27,600
Those in the northern hemisphere realized southern winds were generally warmer than

36
00:03:27,601 --> 00:03:30,160
northern winds.

37
00:03:30,161 --> 00:03:32,240
Dark clouds meant rain.

38
00:03:32,241 --> 00:03:34,240
Lightning preceded thunder.

39
00:03:34,241 --> 00:03:44,800
With the emergence of early civilizations predicting the weather often meant interpreting the will of God.

40
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:50,800
The belief that God would use weather to punish humanity permeates the world's mythologies.

41
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:58,280
A mid-eastern myth, the Gilgamesh epic is perhaps the oldest known work of literature.

42
00:03:58,281 --> 00:04:04,120
conscribed on stone tablets dating back at least 4,100 years.

43
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:08,980
It tells of a life-ending flood along the banks of the Euphrates River.

44
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:13,380
The devastation was brought on by angry gods

45
00:04:13,381 --> 00:04:16,660
who could no longer tolerate the clamor of human beings.

46
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:21,600
A small band of survivors were led by a man named Utnapishtim

47
00:04:21,601 --> 00:04:25,600
who was warned of the devastation by a sympathetic god.

48
00:04:25,601 --> 00:04:30,820
The god instructed Utnapishtim to build a great ark

49
00:04:30,821 --> 00:04:34,520
and to set sail at the first sign of storm clouds

50
00:04:34,521 --> 00:04:39,660
The biblical story of Noah dates back to the same time period

51
00:04:39,661 --> 00:04:41,880
and bears striking similarities

52
00:04:41,881 --> 00:04:46,300
Much of Greek mythology originated with Homer

53
00:04:46,301 --> 00:04:50,300
a blind poet believed to have lived in the 12th century BC

54
00:04:50,301 --> 00:04:55,640
His epic poem, The Odyssey, is full of weather references.

55
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:02,680
He describes the supreme god Zeus as the cloud-bringer,

56
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:07,480
who would vent his fury by casting bolts of lightning toward errant humans.

57
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:12,200
There were probably two aspects to the Greek's view of weather.

58
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:19,680
One was the purely mythical, religious one of gods controlling the weather.

59
00:05:19,681 --> 00:05:23,240
and Zeus and the thunderbolts and these kinds of things.

60
00:05:23,241 --> 00:05:27,660
But there were a number of very perceptive natural philosophers

61
00:05:27,661 --> 00:05:30,840
in the 500 or 600 years before Christ,

62
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:33,440
getting away from the idea that God controlled everything

63
00:05:33,441 --> 00:05:36,940
and it was totally unpredictable and non-deterministic.

64
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:41,860
A natural philosopher named Axe Boris,

65
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:44,240
who lived during the 4th century B.C.,

66
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:48,060
showed uncanny insight on the formation of hail.

67
00:05:48,061 --> 00:05:56,860
He surmised that water vapor would be pushed by warm surface air up to the cold upper regions of the atmosphere.

68
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:04,920
There, individual drops would repeatedly melt and refreeze as they circulated through clouds.

69
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:13,920
Once they were too large and numerous to be carried by air currents, they would fall to the earth as hailstones.

70
00:06:13,921 --> 00:06:23,200
that's almost a textbook explanation of how how hail is formed today and how he did this without

71
00:06:23,201 --> 00:06:30,400
any direct observations of the upper atmosphere is somewhat mysterious aristotle also took great

72
00:06:30,401 --> 00:06:37,780
interest in the weather in 340 bc he recorded his theories about the atmosphere in a book called

73
00:06:37,781 --> 00:06:39,034
meteorologica.

74
00:06:39,035 --> 00:06:45,660
From this title came the word meteorology, meaning the study of atmosphere

75
00:06:45,661 --> 00:06:52,110
and weather prediction. Most of Aristotle's ideas about weather proved wrong.

76
00:06:52,111 --> 00:06:52,811
However,

77
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:57,082
his mere desire to understand it was significant.

78
00:06:57,083 --> 00:07:00,820
The real importance of Aristotle was that he

79
00:07:00,821 --> 00:07:05,700
emphasized the whole time that the way you understand the world around you is to look at it,

80
00:07:05,701 --> 00:07:14,440
Never mind what is written down, just look at what you see going on and try to figure out why it happens the way it does.

81
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:19,620
This was the beginning of what we nowadays call science.

82
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:27,520
200 years after Aristotle in the first century BC, science and religion came together in Athens.

83
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:33,440
The result was perhaps the world's first weather station called the Tower of the Winds.

84
00:07:34,380 --> 00:07:37,140
It was eight-sided, an octagonal tower,

85
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:40,220
and on each of the eight walls at the top of the wall,

86
00:07:40,221 --> 00:07:43,280
there was a carving representing a god,

87
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:46,240
one of the Greek gods associated with a particular kind of weather.

88
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:51,180
On top of the tower, there was a weathervane representing Triton.

89
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:52,760
Now, he was a god of the sea.

90
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,480
And according to the way the weathervane pointed,

91
00:07:56,940 --> 00:07:59,140
you could see what kind of weather you ought to expect.

92
00:07:59,141 --> 00:08:04,080
When the ancient Greeks began to ponder the weather in scientific terms,

93
00:08:04,660 --> 00:08:06,880
they opened a door to the future.

94
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:12,740
Even if weather was ultimately controlled by the gods,

95
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:18,080
perhaps certain aspects of it could be understood or even foreseen by mortals.

96
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:22,240
During Roman times and through the Middle Ages,

97
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:27,600
scientists and philosophers sought to learn more about the primary elements of weather.

98
00:08:27,601 --> 00:08:29,980
water and air.

99
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:36,140
It led to the idea that the atmosphere,

100
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:39,300
this envelope of air in which the earth is contained,

101
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:41,340
the whole of the atmosphere has mass

102
00:08:41,341 --> 00:08:45,180
and this mass exerts weight,

103
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:48,600
the weight of the atmosphere is felt at the surface.

104
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:52,920
While this knowledge did not help anyone to better predict the weather,

105
00:08:52,921 --> 00:08:56,700
it prompted a scientific quest to measure the pressure

106
00:08:56,701 --> 00:08:58,260
exerted by the atmosphere.

107
00:09:02,340 --> 00:09:05,760
Evangelista Torricelli worked as an assistant to Galileo

108
00:09:05,761 --> 00:09:08,640
during the great scientist's final years of life.

109
00:09:09,300 --> 00:09:12,780
In 1643, with the knowledge that air was a substance,

110
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:16,160
Torricelli sought to prove it had weight.

111
00:09:17,420 --> 00:09:21,840
He did this by experimenting with liquid mercury and glass tubes.

112
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:26,500
He submerged the open end of a mercury-filled tube

113
00:09:26,501 --> 00:09:29,460
into a basin that was also filled with mercury.

114
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:35,060
The mercury in the tube could not completely escape

115
00:09:35,061 --> 00:09:37,740
because as Torricelli correctly reasoned,

116
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:41,160
air was exerting pressure on the mercury in the basin,

117
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:43,500
forcing some of it back up the tube.

118
00:09:46,660 --> 00:09:50,200
Torricelli's experiment led to his invention of the barometer,

119
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,380
an instrument capable of measuring the weight of air.

120
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:57,840
He proved what he set out to prove, which is that air has weight,

121
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:01,060
and in doing so, he'd invented the barometer.

122
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:04,660
And it's basically the barometer we still use today.

123
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:10,340
Shortly afterwards, the link between barometric pressure and weather was made.

124
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:14,960
Mercury always dropped right before a storm,

125
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:17,280
signaling a system of low pressure.

126
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:23,040
Since 1665, most barometers have been labeled with words or symbols

127
00:10:23,041 --> 00:10:26,540
to indicate stormy or mild weather conditions.

128
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:32,140
Even before Torricelli invented the barometer,

129
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:36,060
mathematicians sought ways of calculating temperature.

130
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:39,900
Yet the invention of an accurate mercury thermometer

131
00:10:39,901 --> 00:10:42,320
would be accomplished not by a great scientist,

132
00:10:43,040 --> 00:10:47,440
but by a young Amsterdam glassblower in 1714.

133
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:50,500
His name was Gabriel Fahrenheit.

134
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:52,060
This was not unusual.

135
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,360
People who invented things were usually craftsmen of one sort or another.

136
00:10:57,361 --> 00:11:00,260
The kind of theoretical scientists that we know about today

137
00:11:00,261 --> 00:11:04,540
was still a little way off.

138
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:08,120
And a lot of the really useful work was done by craftspeople.

139
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:11,540
Fahrenheit found that a mixture of ice and salt

140
00:11:11,541 --> 00:11:15,420
resulted in the coldest possible temperature he could produce.

141
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:19,760
For simplicity, he chose to use positive numbers

142
00:11:19,761 --> 00:11:21,460
for warmer temperature readings.

143
00:11:22,060 --> 00:11:26,060
So he labeled this coldest point zero.

144
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:32,560
Fahrenheit based his temperature scale on measurable units of heat and coldness called degrees.

145
00:11:33,940 --> 00:11:39,480
He found that water turned to ice at exactly 32 degrees above zero.

146
00:11:41,460 --> 00:11:45,000
Thus, 32 degrees became his freezing point.

147
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:51,680
A third instrument, still used today, was also invented in the 1700s.

148
00:11:52,060 --> 00:11:57,340
The hygrometer measures air humidity and does so in an unexpected manner.

149
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:01,720
Well, the earliest hygrometers were based on the expansion of the human hair,

150
00:12:01,721 --> 00:12:05,220
especially long hair would get longer with the high humidity

151
00:12:05,221 --> 00:12:10,140
and then shrink up as the humidity dried out.

152
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:16,400
Then they found that you could attach a human hair to essentially a spring or a pointer

153
00:12:16,401 --> 00:12:22,180
and measure the displacement of the hair as the humidity changed.

154
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:26,540
After thousands of years of pondering the weather,

155
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:31,800
it would be the short span of seven decades from 1643 to 1714

156
00:12:31,801 --> 00:12:34,700
that would produce the basic instruments

157
00:12:34,701 --> 00:12:38,500
to make future scientific weather forecasting possible.

158
00:12:38,501 --> 00:12:50,060
In 2200 BC, a severe drought brought the collapse of Akkad,

159
00:12:50,061 --> 00:12:54,140
the Egyptian kingdom responsible for the greatest pyramids.

160
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:58,140
Weather prediction will return on Modern Marvels.

161
00:12:58,141 --> 00:13:10,160
Nowhere in the world are there as many severe forms of weather as in the United States.

162
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:14,160
Each year Americans can count on more than a thousand tornadoes,

163
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:17,520
5,000 floods,

164
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:22,240
and 10,000 severe thunderstorms.

165
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:25,160
The country will also be battered by autumn hurricanes,

166
00:13:25,161 --> 00:13:32,160
hurricanes, winter blizzards, and summer heat waves.

167
00:13:32,161 --> 00:13:39,160
America has a history rich in weather.

168
00:13:39,161 --> 00:13:44,160
It began with elaborate rituals performed by Native Americans

169
00:13:44,161 --> 00:13:46,160
to influence the weather.

170
00:13:46,161 --> 00:13:51,160
I'd say the majority of the native rituals of the southwest,

171
00:13:51,161 --> 00:13:54,460
southwest, the Pueblo Indians, are weather-related.

172
00:13:54,880 --> 00:14:00,060
And most of them are rituals whose purpose is to bring rain.

173
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:03,780
One of these rituals had the potential to be deadly.

174
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:06,160
It was called the snake dance.

175
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,280
A certain portion of the tribe will use snakes by handling them

176
00:14:11,281 --> 00:14:13,400
and also putting the snakes in their mouths.

177
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:18,480
And my sense is that the danger and the risk involved in that

178
00:14:18,481 --> 00:14:23,860
as a way of entering more deeply into an attempt to commune with nature

179
00:14:23,861 --> 00:14:28,820
and control the uncontrollable and the power of nature, which will bring the rain.

180
00:14:29,620 --> 00:14:33,060
In recent decades, the dancers used non-venomous bull snakes.

181
00:14:33,960 --> 00:14:38,260
But originally, the dance was performed with poisonous rattlesnakes.

182
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:43,880
The rattles were thought to be symbolic of thunder,

183
00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:48,300
while the snake's diamond-shaped skin pattern represented lightning,

184
00:14:48,480 --> 00:15:01,260
In the 1770s, as America sought its independence from England,

185
00:15:01,740 --> 00:15:05,300
understanding weather was as important for knowing when to plant crops

186
00:15:05,301 --> 00:15:08,360
as it was for knowing when to go into battle.

187
00:15:11,580 --> 00:15:14,780
George Washington believed the key to predicting weather

188
00:15:14,781 --> 00:15:17,460
was keeping meticulous records of its behavior

189
00:15:17,461 --> 00:15:19,340
and looking for patterns.

190
00:15:20,540 --> 00:15:23,840
I'm fascinated by how interested,

191
00:15:24,240 --> 00:15:26,280
and I would even use the term obsessed,

192
00:15:26,820 --> 00:15:29,240
some of the founding fathers were with weather.

193
00:15:30,320 --> 00:15:32,440
Washington kept a weather journal

194
00:15:32,441 --> 00:15:33,880
and kept track of what it was doing,

195
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,840
but Jefferson is the real hero

196
00:15:35,841 --> 00:15:39,880
of enlightenment, weather, observations.

197
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:44,220
Thomas Jefferson kept extensive daily records

198
00:15:44,221 --> 00:15:47,060
on temperature, barometric pressure,

199
00:15:47,061 --> 00:15:48,640
and other weather conditions.

200
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:53,780
He recorded events such as the earliest date of Bud Open,

201
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:57,440
or when bees first appeared in his orchard.

202
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:01,900
He encouraged other colonial leaders to do the same

203
00:16:01,901 --> 00:16:06,520
in order to detect patterns in how weather moved across the colonies,

204
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:11,320
an idea that is at the core of today's national weather service.

205
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:15,460
Benjamin Franklin was also fascinated by weather.

206
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:21,020
He saw in it the secrets to greater scientific understanding of the natural world.

207
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:27,600
His famous kite and key experiment was an attempt to understand what lightning was.

208
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:32,800
In a sense, he became America's first storm chaser.

209
00:16:33,620 --> 00:16:37,020
Those enthusiastic scientists and curiosity seekers

210
00:16:37,021 --> 00:16:41,440
who get as close as they can to tornadoes or other severe forms of weather.

211
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:45,140
Franklin chased lightning.

212
00:16:45,460 --> 00:16:59,460
Franklin really was a scientist and he was determined to prove that lightning was not some emanation from God's eyeballs or a form of mystical punishment or what have

213
00:17:00,170 --> 00:17:03,980
you, but that it was a form of electricity.

214
00:17:03,981 --> 00:17:08,600
With this knowledge, Franklin invented the lightning rod.

215
00:17:09,640 --> 00:17:14,420
He correctly surmised that if a pointed metal rod with wires leading to the ground

216
00:17:14,421 --> 00:17:16,680
were attached to the highest points of buildings,

217
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:22,940
lightning would strike the rod and its deadly charge would be diverted into the ground.

218
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:29,740
Churches with their high steeples were more susceptible than any other building to attracting lightning.

219
00:17:30,540 --> 00:17:33,860
Yet many church leaders resisted lightning rods.

220
00:17:33,980 --> 00:17:38,700
To put up a lightning rod would be going against God's will.

221
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:44,360
If God wanted to strike the church or the house with lightning, there must be a reason.

222
00:17:45,220 --> 00:17:49,200
Ironically, while such beliefs frustrated and outraged Franklin,

223
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:54,600
his annual weather predictions in Poor Richard's Almanac were extremely popular.

224
00:17:56,280 --> 00:18:00,140
They were usually not very specific and often amusing.

225
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:03,640
July 19, dog days begin.

226
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:07,080
August 21, Dog Day's end.

227
00:18:08,040 --> 00:18:10,540
Yet from its first printing in 1732,

228
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:15,280
the almanac averaged sales of 10,000 per issue.

229
00:18:16,120 --> 00:18:18,640
A staggering circulation for the time.

230
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:24,420
The word almanac is from an Arabic root meaning calendar of the heavens.

231
00:18:26,180 --> 00:18:29,900
In 1792, during George Washington's presidency,

232
00:18:30,180 --> 00:18:33,520
more almanacs were being published than newspapers.

233
00:18:33,980 --> 00:18:38,020
and the newest was called the Old Farmer's Almanac.

234
00:18:38,940 --> 00:18:42,340
Today it's the longest-running publication in America.

235
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:49,640
The Old Farmer's Almanac, I think, makes a kind of anchor in times when we're anxious about the future.

236
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:56,360
It goes back to a time, not just generations, our parents and grandparents,

237
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:00,740
but really the beginnings of our country, and back beyond that,

238
00:19:00,741 --> 00:19:05,060
to the beginnings of people looking up at the sky and trying to make sense of what was happening there.

239
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:09,440
Full of recipes, tips, and trivia on hundreds of topics,

240
00:19:09,940 --> 00:19:14,000
one of the elements that has made the old Farmer's Almanac endure

241
00:19:14,001 --> 00:19:20,460
are the annual forecasts that originated with its founder and 50-year editor, Robert B.

242
00:19:20,461 --> 00:19:21,161
Thomas.

243
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:29,160
Robert Thomas' forecasts were based in part on the 11-year cycle of sunspot activity

244
00:19:29,161 --> 00:19:32,100
first observed by Leonardo da Vinci.

245
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:37,760
The solar cycle is still used by the Almanac today.

246
00:19:38,680 --> 00:19:43,080
It's not quite as simple as saying the weather's going to repeat itself every 11 years.

247
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:45,720
But knowing what year of the cycle we're in

248
00:19:45,721 --> 00:19:49,240
and then factoring in current weather conditions all over the world,

249
00:19:49,500 --> 00:19:53,080
we can put together a forecast that we feel pretty confident will hold up for a year.

250
00:19:53,081 --> 00:19:59,500
The almanac's long presence in America has endowed it with an aura of accuracy.

251
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:01,560
People want us to be right.

252
00:20:02,100 --> 00:20:05,060
They want to believe that there is something older than science,

253
00:20:05,460 --> 00:20:09,980
that we have tapped into some mythic reservoir of information about the universe,

254
00:20:10,900 --> 00:20:12,320
and they find that reassuring.

255
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:19,060
The Old Farmer's Almanac serves as a historical bridge between the days of weather lore

256
00:20:19,061 --> 00:20:28,000
and the explosion of technology that was to make weather prediction of global endeavor and life-saving science.

257
00:20:30,260 --> 00:20:38,900
The first two men who attempted to replicate Benjamin Franklin's Tide and Key experiment were both struck by lightning and killed.

258
00:20:39,880 --> 00:20:42,800
Weather prediction will return on Modern Marvels.

259
00:20:42,801 --> 00:20:52,060
On September 1st, 1938, a hurricane turned the southeastern United States into a watery death trap.

260
00:20:52,900 --> 00:21:00,680
With more than 600 dead, 30,000 injured, and 16,000 homes and businesses lost.

261
00:21:01,680 --> 00:21:09,440
It was a bigger disaster than either the 1871 Chicago Fire or 1906 San Francisco earthquake.

262
00:21:09,441 --> 00:21:16,080
Today, weather satellites would have tracked such a hurricane from the time it formed.

263
00:21:17,520 --> 00:21:20,560
Evacuation warnings would be issued days in advance.

264
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:23,940
Only those who failed to heed the warnings would be at risk.

265
00:21:26,120 --> 00:21:34,060
Prior to the mid-19th century, word of a fast-moving storm could travel only as quickly as a messenger could carry it.

266
00:21:34,061 --> 00:21:39,560
In 1844, the invention of the telegraph changed that.

267
00:21:39,561 --> 00:21:45,560
That was the major breakthrough in terms of weather prediction,

268
00:21:45,561 --> 00:21:48,560
because if you knew what it was doing in Kansas,

269
00:21:48,561 --> 00:21:53,060
you had a pretty good idea of what it would soon be doing in areas to the east.

270
00:21:53,061 --> 00:21:58,560
At the time, Joseph Henry was the first director of the Smithsonian Institution.

271
00:21:58,561 --> 00:22:04,140
Henry used the telegraph to create a nationwide network of weather observers.

272
00:22:05,140 --> 00:22:11,520
He distributed thermometers, barometers, and other instruments to volunteers across the country.

273
00:22:12,680 --> 00:22:17,900
The observers would take daily readings and then telegraph them to Henry at the Smithsonian.

274
00:22:17,901 --> 00:22:20,500
And he had tons of people who volunteered.

275
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:27,640
There were lots of weather nets back then who were only too happy to spend the time to gather the data and report it to him.

276
00:22:27,641 --> 00:22:36,040
So it was the first scientific weather network that covered the country.

277
00:22:36,380 --> 00:22:40,080
And in fact, it was really the forerunner of the National Weather Service.

278
00:22:40,620 --> 00:22:43,840
While Henry's system provided a national picture of weather,

279
00:22:44,660 --> 00:22:47,180
ground-based observations had limitations.

280
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:53,760
Devastating weather events could destroy cities and take lives with little or no warning.

281
00:22:53,761 --> 00:22:58,760
Such was the case with the 1900 Galveston, Texas, hurricane.

282
00:22:59,520 --> 00:23:04,340
It killed 6,000 people and destroyed half the island's buildings.

283
00:23:05,420 --> 00:23:10,640
Before such tragedies could be averted, weather would have to be studied where it began,

284
00:23:11,440 --> 00:23:14,160
more than 20,000 feet above the ground.

285
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:20,120
Meteorologists had been taking atmospheric readings since the early 1900s

286
00:23:20,121 --> 00:23:22,980
by attaching weather instrument packages to balloons.

287
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:28,120
and releasing them into the atmosphere.

288
00:23:28,121 --> 00:23:33,780
The emergence of radio greatly increased the effectiveness of weather balloons.

289
00:23:33,781 --> 00:23:37,500
Radio transmitters were attached to the instrument packages

290
00:23:37,501 --> 00:23:40,160
called radiosondes.

291
00:23:40,161 --> 00:23:44,580
They delivered real-time readings of the upper atmosphere to meteorologists

292
00:23:44,581 --> 00:23:48,440
on the ground.

293
00:23:48,441 --> 00:23:52,420
And that was invaluable in predicting weather because obviously

294
00:23:52,421 --> 00:23:57,820
what the upper atmosphere is doing is far more important in terms of general circulation

295
00:23:57,821 --> 00:24:01,140
and the longer-term patterns than what it's doing on the ground.

296
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:06,320
During World War I, the Radiosonde helped a Norwegian meteorologist,

297
00:24:06,780 --> 00:24:12,020
Wilhelm Björknes, realize that enormous masses of air with similar temperature

298
00:24:12,021 --> 00:24:16,380
and barometric pressure were constantly moving through the atmosphere.

299
00:24:16,381 --> 00:24:25,380
He described how warm and cold air masses advanced, retreated, and battled for superiority when they made contact.

300
00:24:26,380 --> 00:24:35,380
The result would be either the clear mild days associated with high pressure, or harsh storms associated with low pressure.

301
00:24:35,381 --> 00:24:36,081
pressure.

302
00:24:36,082 --> 00:24:42,060
With images of the bloody fighting that occurred at the front lines of the war

303
00:24:42,061 --> 00:24:48,590
in his mind, Björk Nies called these dueling air masses fronts.

304
00:24:48,591 --> 00:24:51,700
He believed that understanding

305
00:24:51,701 --> 00:24:54,840
fronts was the key to improved weather forecasting.

306
00:24:54,841 --> 00:25:02,380
His greatest contribution was the realization that the weather, the atmosphere, behaved

307
00:25:02,381 --> 00:25:10,020
as a fluid and in principle the future evolution of the atmosphere could be solved

308
00:25:10,021 --> 00:25:16,880
by equations which start out with the existing state of the atmosphere and then extrapolate

309
00:25:16,881 --> 00:25:19,523
forward in time to get future states.

310
00:25:19,524 --> 00:25:23,380
So tomorrow's weather depends only on today's weather.

311
00:25:23,381 --> 00:25:29,210
Björkneys was on to an idea that decades later would revolutionize forecasting.

312
00:25:29,211 --> 00:25:29,911
A system

313
00:25:29,912 --> 00:25:34,960
of numerical equations that could analyze current weather to predict future weather.

314
00:25:35,940 --> 00:25:40,760
What Bjergnes lacked was the necessary computer power to make such equations.

315
00:25:42,420 --> 00:25:46,320
However, a British mathematician, Louis Fry Richardson, came close.

316
00:25:47,420 --> 00:25:52,660
A pacifist Quaker, Richardson served as an ambulance driver during World War I.

317
00:25:53,240 --> 00:25:56,340
In between driving the ambulance, he worked out these equations

318
00:25:56,341 --> 00:26:04,240
to actually predict the atmosphere ahead of time into the future based on the initial state.

319
00:26:04,241 --> 00:26:08,020
And he wrote down the equations that were very similar to what we use today.

320
00:26:08,920 --> 00:26:13,200
Richardson spent weeks doing complex calculations by hand.

321
00:26:13,820 --> 00:26:19,660
They involved applying the laws of physics to the rise and fall of atmospheric pressure on the surface.

322
00:26:20,500 --> 00:26:25,020
When he finally made a forecast, it turned out to be a disappointing failure.

323
00:26:25,021 --> 00:26:31,140
Now we know almost a century later why he failed.

324
00:26:31,141 --> 00:26:34,300
It wasn't because the idea was bad at all. The idea was terrific.

325
00:26:35,100 --> 00:26:36,700
In fact, his equations were really good.

326
00:26:37,480 --> 00:26:43,500
He was just using, he didn't have enough data, particularly in the upper atmosphere above the surface,

327
00:26:43,980 --> 00:26:48,320
where much of the, where actually the most important atmospheric changes occurred.

328
00:26:49,060 --> 00:26:52,080
It would be decades before such data could be gathered.

329
00:26:52,940 --> 00:26:55,800
Aviation would provide an important first step.

330
00:26:56,560 --> 00:26:59,340
While World War I led to the discovery of France,

331
00:27:00,220 --> 00:27:05,040
World War II would reveal the mysterious force that helped propel them.

332
00:27:06,320 --> 00:27:08,880
American pilots attempting to cross the Pacific

333
00:27:08,881 --> 00:27:12,980
traveled at a maximum airspeed of 200 miles per hour.

334
00:27:13,800 --> 00:27:16,680
Occasionally, they would find themselves slowing down

335
00:27:16,681 --> 00:27:20,680
or actually moving backwards relative to the ocean.

336
00:27:21,540 --> 00:27:27,000
They came to realise this phenomenon was being caused by a narrow and powerful ribbon of air.

337
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:29,900
They called it the jet stream.

338
00:27:30,640 --> 00:27:34,320
And now we know, again, we know a lot more about the jet stream,

339
00:27:34,360 --> 00:27:39,640
and one of the things that we know about it is that it develops waves that move along it,

340
00:27:40,060 --> 00:27:43,740
and depressions, areas of atmospheric low pressure,

341
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:48,580
form beneath parts of these waves and get dragged along with them.

342
00:27:48,581 --> 00:27:57,100
In 1944, such a mass of low pressure was responsible for a hurricane that bore down on Miami, Florida.

343
00:27:57,980 --> 00:28:03,300
But this time, scientists would gain insight into the atmospheric conditions of hurricanes.

344
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:14,700
A sturdy B-29 bomber used only months earlier over Germany in the war would be the first to fly through the eye of a hurricane.

345
00:28:14,701 --> 00:28:24,880
The crew, buffeted by the powerful swirling winds, took temperature, air pressure and wind speed readings before landing safely on the ground.

346
00:28:27,060 --> 00:28:35,940
While military aircraft opened new frontiers for meteorology, the most important weather technology to come out of the war was radar.

347
00:28:36,960 --> 00:28:44,080
Radar gave the Allies the tremendous advantage of detecting German battleships and aircraft from miles away.

348
00:28:44,700 --> 00:28:51,700
American and British operators noticed that radar could also detect rainstorms.

349
00:28:51,701 --> 00:28:58,700
Immediately after World War II, very quickly researchers got involved with this observing system

350
00:28:58,701 --> 00:29:06,700
and its ability to observe rain and then snow under certain circumstances

351
00:29:06,701 --> 00:29:11,700
and be able to use that to map out where the precipitation was coming from,

352
00:29:11,701 --> 00:29:13,700
how it was evolving and where it was going to.

353
00:29:14,700 --> 00:29:20,600
In 1957, the National Weather Service's network of more than 100 weather stations

354
00:29:20,601 --> 00:29:23,320
installed radar at each site.

355
00:29:25,220 --> 00:29:28,600
Nationwide weather observation was now greatly enhanced

356
00:29:28,601 --> 00:29:32,100
by the power of these large electronic eyes.

357
00:29:33,140 --> 00:29:37,220
Twenty years later, radar would see even deeper.

358
00:29:37,221 --> 00:29:45,260
when Anders Celsius proposed a new temperature scale in 1742

359
00:29:45,261 --> 00:29:49,260
he suggested that the freezing point be 100 degrees

360
00:29:49,261 --> 00:29:51,360
and zero for boiling

361
00:29:51,361 --> 00:29:55,260
weather prediction will return on Modern Marvels

362
00:29:59,340 --> 00:30:00,920
coming right towards us

363
00:30:00,921 --> 00:30:03,860
get ready to go downstairs

364
00:30:03,861 --> 00:30:07,040
go turn TV off, forget the dog

365
00:30:07,041 --> 00:30:14,040
Tornadoes, more than any other deadly weather event, strike with the least amount of warning.

366
00:30:14,041 --> 00:30:19,040
They account for one half of all weather-related deaths.

367
00:30:19,041 --> 00:30:26,040
Prior to the 1990s, warnings could not be issued until a tornado actually touched down.

368
00:30:26,041 --> 00:30:32,040
The National Weather Service had what I would call reactive warnings.

369
00:30:32,041 --> 00:30:41,940
When there was an observed tornado on the ground, we put the warning out for that area and those areas downstream of that particular cloud mass.

370
00:30:42,740 --> 00:30:51,120
Research begun in the 1970s led to a new type of radar, one that could detect tornadoes before they formed.

371
00:30:51,121 --> 00:31:03,120
It's called Doppler radar, and in the early 1990s, Doppler was installed at all 118 National Weather Service stations across the United States.

372
00:31:04,480 --> 00:31:16,000
With the new radars, we're able to provide warnings before the tornado actually forms and starts extending towards the ground.

373
00:31:16,001 --> 00:31:21,740
By doing that, we have an average of a 12-minute lead time for tornado warnings.

374
00:31:22,160 --> 00:31:29,940
12 minutes is a lot of time when you need to get out or get under a protective area.

375
00:31:30,940 --> 00:31:37,920
While conventional radar merely recognizes falling rain or cloud density, Doppler goes further.

376
00:31:38,920 --> 00:31:41,820
It can measure the amount of moisture within a cloud.

377
00:31:41,821 --> 00:31:46,820
It can distinguish between warm and cool air within a weather system.

378
00:31:46,821 --> 00:31:52,820
Doppler can track the movement of storm elements such as raindrops or dust particles.

379
00:31:52,821 --> 00:31:57,820
It can also measure wind direction and velocity.

380
00:31:57,821 --> 00:32:06,820
This has enormous implications because if you're measuring the wind field in storm systems such as a convective cloud mass,

381
00:32:06,821 --> 00:32:15,420
And if the wind field is beginning to rotate, then you might be able to infer where there is a tornado.

382
00:32:15,800 --> 00:32:23,820
And we have powerful computers now hooked up with the radar system that take those shifts and convert them into motion.

383
00:32:25,220 --> 00:32:35,980
The mid-1990s development of mobile Doppler radar allowed daring meteorologists known as storm chasers to study tornado formation up close.

384
00:32:36,820 --> 00:32:44,140
These truck-mounted Dopplers are most often used in Tornado Alley,

385
00:32:44,960 --> 00:32:48,740
a stretch of the central United States running from Texas to Iowa.

386
00:32:49,540 --> 00:32:52,360
It's where the majority of tornadoes strike.

387
00:32:55,500 --> 00:32:58,100
The storm chasers go out to the weather.

388
00:32:58,620 --> 00:32:59,640
They're event-driven.

389
00:33:00,420 --> 00:33:04,080
These are some of your most brilliant meteorologists out there,

390
00:33:04,081 --> 00:33:10,860
getting those observations that will help them understand how these storms are evolved.

391
00:33:10,980 --> 00:33:13,460
It's not just going out for the thrill of the observation.

392
00:33:14,580 --> 00:33:20,000
That better understanding then factors in to our ability to warn more efficiently

393
00:33:20,001 --> 00:33:22,300
whether a storm is tornadic or not.

394
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:26,560
However, some weather events require more than Doppler.

395
00:33:27,960 --> 00:33:32,920
Prior to the 1960s, when deadly weather such as hurricanes or typhoons

396
00:33:32,921 --> 00:33:39,623
formed over the ocean, they were almost impossible to detect.

397
00:33:39,624 --> 00:33:42,920
This 1959 typhoon left 100,000

398
00:33:42,921 --> 00:33:49,680
people homeless on the islands of India's Ganges Delta. Though by the 1960s, weather

399
00:33:49,681 --> 00:33:52,570
observation would rise to new heights.

400
00:33:52,571 --> 00:33:56,600
Satellites would give the world an unprecedented view

401
00:33:56,601 --> 00:33:58,287
of the atmosphere.

402
00:33:58,288 --> 00:34:04,940
On April 1, 1960, a 21-pound satellite called TIROS-1 was launched into

403
00:34:04,941 --> 00:34:07,609
orbit over the United States.

404
00:34:07,610 --> 00:34:13,040
TIROS, short for Television Infrared Observation Satellite,

405
00:34:13,041 --> 00:34:16,770
was the world's first weather satellite.

406
00:34:16,771 --> 00:34:21,340
Its two photoelectric cells served as outer space

407
00:34:21,341 --> 00:34:25,340
orbiting at about 500 miles above the earth,

408
00:34:25,341 --> 00:34:29,340
Tiros recorded images of Earth on a magnetic recorder

409
00:34:29,341 --> 00:34:33,340
and transmitted them back.

410
00:34:33,341 --> 00:34:37,340
The world finally had a global view of weather.

411
00:34:37,341 --> 00:34:43,340
But like radar, satellite imagery would soon become clearer, stronger, and better.

412
00:34:45,340 --> 00:34:49,340
The first geostationary operational environmental satellite

413
00:34:49,341 --> 00:34:53,660
Environmental Satellite, or GOES, was launched in 1974.

414
00:34:57,500 --> 00:35:02,460
Geostationary means that the satellite orbits at a single fixed point relative to the Earth.

415
00:35:03,100 --> 00:35:09,860
At a height of 25,000 miles, two satellites offer a blanket of coverage to the United States.

416
00:35:10,480 --> 00:35:13,520
So that's very important from a weather forecasting standpoint,

417
00:35:14,140 --> 00:35:18,480
because we can look at the complete evolution of clouds, cloud systems, and storms.

418
00:35:18,481 --> 00:35:21,600
We're now able to actually see water vapor, which is very important.

419
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:23,760
As you can imagine, without water vapor, we can't have rain.

420
00:35:24,320 --> 00:35:27,400
So it's relative humidity or water vapor in the atmosphere

421
00:35:27,401 --> 00:35:31,600
that is one of the ingredients in producing rain, snow, other precipitation.

422
00:35:32,220 --> 00:35:37,160
This ability allows the satellites to spot hurricanes in the earliest stages.

423
00:35:37,960 --> 00:35:41,700
They often blow through the Caribbean and southeastern United States

424
00:35:41,701 --> 00:35:44,620
after forming on the African coast.

425
00:35:44,621 --> 00:35:54,360
We can follow the specific development phases of these storms and their movement for about 7 to 10 days.

426
00:35:55,660 --> 00:36:02,060
This is an incredibly important period of time for us to be able to get the information on these storms

427
00:36:02,061 --> 00:36:05,800
because the more data you collect earlier on in the process,

428
00:36:06,280 --> 00:36:10,260
the better your forecasts become as these storms approach the United States.

429
00:36:10,261 --> 00:36:16,460
Yet hurricanes are erratic enough that pinpointing a landfall mark is still difficult.

430
00:36:17,180 --> 00:36:22,460
But the next advance in meteorology, the computer weather model, seeks to change that.

431
00:36:24,940 --> 00:36:31,640
In 1920, Seminole chief Billy Bowlegs evacuated his tribe from the Florida Everglades

432
00:36:31,641 --> 00:36:36,460
after using animal behavior to correctly predict a hurricane.

433
00:36:37,600 --> 00:36:39,560
Modern marvels will return.

434
00:36:40,260 --> 00:36:46,280
Well, the poor old weatherman who comes in for a lot of criticism ought to be fairly popular today,

435
00:36:46,360 --> 00:36:51,320
at least 75% popular because we've got at least 75% good weather.

436
00:36:51,840 --> 00:36:58,920
Since the 1950s, perhaps nobody has been as closely associated with meteorology than television weathermen.

437
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:04,260
While the subject matter involves nature's most powerful force,

438
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:07,880
weathermen have had a long tradition of being entertainers,

439
00:37:07,881 --> 00:37:11,940
attempting to outdo each other with flashy maps and gags.

440
00:37:12,760 --> 00:37:14,460
It's been called the longest running joke.

441
00:37:14,660 --> 00:37:16,020
They've used every prank.

442
00:37:16,180 --> 00:37:18,500
I mean, there have been pigs, people standing on their heads,

443
00:37:18,960 --> 00:37:20,660
funny cartoon characters.

444
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:24,180
Tex Antoine, one of the first TV weathermen

445
00:37:24,181 --> 00:37:27,040
delivered his reports in a scientist's smock

446
00:37:27,041 --> 00:37:31,100
with the help of Uncle Weatherby, a wall-mounted puppet.

447
00:37:31,480 --> 00:37:33,660
Setting up, we've had the first snowfall at Sierra Summit,

448
00:37:33,740 --> 00:37:34,580
an inch of snow up there.

449
00:37:34,581 --> 00:37:36,400
It's way too early for this kind of thing.

450
00:37:36,820 --> 00:37:37,520
What does that mean?

451
00:37:37,880 --> 00:37:38,940
I don't know.

452
00:37:39,880 --> 00:37:46,320
Fritz Coleman was hired 15 years ago after being discovered on the stage of a nightclub doing stand-up comedy.

453
00:37:47,320 --> 00:37:52,600
But today, weather reporting, while still often entertaining, has grown more serious.

454
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:59,520
Coleman, like other weather forecasters, receives up-to-the-minute data with which to develop his forecast.

455
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:07,700
Computers allowed him to isolate any of the National Weather Service's 118 Doppler radar stations

456
00:38:07,701 --> 00:38:11,120
and to then use the visual display in his forecast.

457
00:38:13,900 --> 00:38:17,700
He receives volumes of instant satellite images

458
00:38:17,701 --> 00:38:21,500
and other forecast information from private weather service firms.

459
00:38:22,600 --> 00:38:27,040
One company, AccuWeather, provides some of the brilliant computer graphics

460
00:38:27,041 --> 00:38:32,300
that have turned old-fashioned weather maps into vivid three-dimensional displays.

461
00:38:32,301 --> 00:38:38,980
AccuWeather meteorologists also consult by phone with forecasters before they go on the air.

462
00:38:39,520 --> 00:38:46,240
My job is to take the technical meteorological information

463
00:38:46,241 --> 00:38:51,040
and make it easily understood by the viewing public

464
00:38:51,041 --> 00:38:56,600
because basically they just want to know if they need to take an umbrella to work tomorrow

465
00:38:56,601 --> 00:38:58,160
or if they need a sweater tomorrow

466
00:38:58,161 --> 00:39:01,960
or how is the weather going to affect my child's walk to the bus stop.

467
00:39:02,300 --> 00:39:08,600
Whether the information is gathered from private meteorological firms or the National Weather Service,

468
00:39:09,260 --> 00:39:15,840
nationwide forecasting is the result of a broad spectrum of atmospheric measuring and observing techniques.

469
00:39:19,140 --> 00:39:23,460
National Weather Service sites blanket the country with Doppler radar

470
00:39:23,461 --> 00:39:28,180
and a variety of basic instruments such as barometers and thermometers.

471
00:39:28,181 --> 00:39:35,180
Overhead satellites keep an eye on developing storms around the globe.

472
00:39:35,181 --> 00:39:44,180
Weather balloons are released twice daily from thousands of weather stations throughout the world

473
00:39:44,181 --> 00:39:48,180
and the readings are shared through an international data bank.

474
00:39:48,181 --> 00:39:53,180
Weather readings are even gathered from the huge fleet of commercial aircraft

475
00:39:53,181 --> 00:39:57,180
that circumnavigate the globe every day.

476
00:39:57,181 --> 00:40:02,180
Miniature Doppler radar has been installed on the flight recorders of most jets.

477
00:40:02,181 --> 00:40:07,180
Atmospheric conditions are transmitted to receiver sites on the ground.

478
00:40:07,181 --> 00:40:14,180
Data is collected as a plane takes off and lands, so we can get a vertical profile of the data during that period.

479
00:40:14,181 --> 00:40:17,180
We can get data every five seconds, for example.

480
00:40:17,181 --> 00:40:23,180
And then once it's at a flight level, you can get data either every 15 seconds or every 30 seconds.

481
00:40:23,181 --> 00:40:27,380
and there are central sites throughout the country run by a private company

482
00:40:27,381 --> 00:40:32,240
that actually peels away the meteorological data

483
00:40:32,241 --> 00:40:34,360
and then transmits it to us.

484
00:40:35,100 --> 00:40:38,500
Today, this enormous collection of information

485
00:40:38,501 --> 00:40:42,360
is used to develop computer models of what the weather is doing

486
00:40:42,361 --> 00:40:44,700
and what it is likely to do.

487
00:40:45,580 --> 00:40:48,340
Such models represent the most advanced

488
00:40:48,341 --> 00:40:51,820
and accurate form of forecasting ever developed.

489
00:40:53,180 --> 00:41:01,040
The majority of American computer models are developed at the National Center for Atmospheric Research in Boulder, Colorado.

490
00:41:02,140 --> 00:41:05,880
They are utilized by the National Weather Service to produce forecasts.

491
00:41:07,080 --> 00:41:10,160
Computer models are the product of numerical forecasting,

492
00:41:11,060 --> 00:41:16,680
using equations like those written by World War I ambulance driver Louis Richardson

493
00:41:16,681 --> 00:41:22,120
to calculate future weather conditions based on current conditions.

494
00:41:23,180 --> 00:41:35,380
So the meteorologists over the past 40 or even 50 years have devised ways of representing the atmosphere and now the ocean with these equations in computers.

495
00:41:36,580 --> 00:41:47,993
And we can pose the problem in such a way that we can define an initial state with our current observations, whether they be satellites or balloons or airplanes, and

496
00:41:47,994 --> 00:41:50,760
then step these equations ahead in time.

497
00:41:50,761 --> 00:41:54,880
And that's what a numerical forecast is all about.

498
00:41:55,380 --> 00:41:59,100
Computer models analyze the atmosphere and ocean

499
00:41:59,101 --> 00:42:03,520
as though they were composed of individual balls of air or water.

500
00:42:04,720 --> 00:42:08,660
How these balls move can be an indicator of future weather.

501
00:42:09,620 --> 00:42:12,300
In this case, the model is predicting a hurricane.

502
00:42:14,220 --> 00:42:16,800
Computer weather models are another incarnation

503
00:42:16,801 --> 00:42:18,900
of what human beings have sought to do

504
00:42:18,901 --> 00:42:24,040
since the ancient Greeks built the Tower of the Winds.

505
00:42:24,041 --> 00:42:28,300
To build a device to do what we can't do alone,

506
00:42:28,301 --> 00:42:31,840
to harness the chaos that is weather.

507
00:42:31,841 --> 00:42:35,400
But over the centuries, one constant has remained.

508
00:42:35,401 --> 00:42:39,440
Nature can always trick the best forecaster.

509
00:42:39,441 --> 00:42:42,560
All of the elements in forecasting weather are variable.

510
00:42:42,561 --> 00:42:46,900
Temperature, relative humidity, wind speed, barometric pressure,

511
00:42:46,901 --> 00:42:48,760
they're all constantly changing.

512
00:42:48,761 --> 00:42:50,020
These are acts of God.

513
00:42:50,380 --> 00:42:54,480
And I always say, if I've made a mistake, I'm in sales, not management.

514
00:42:55,620 --> 00:42:57,000
I'm not calling the shots.

515
00:42:57,100 --> 00:42:59,480
I'm just sort of the conduit for the information.

516
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:05,560
It amazed me as a kid that people could even try to forecast the weather.

517
00:43:06,480 --> 00:43:11,460
Now that I see how it's done and all the information that's brought to it,

518
00:43:11,461 --> 00:43:13,080
it doesn't amaze me as much.

519
00:43:15,420 --> 00:43:17,800
But I'm still ready for surprises.

520
00:43:17,801 --> 00:43:24,800
Just when you think that you've got it and that you can really nail this on a routine basis,

521
00:43:25,440 --> 00:43:28,380
Mother Nature comes through with a surprise and it humbles you.

522
00:43:29,080 --> 00:43:35,540
The awesome, chaotic power of weather has not only humbled human beings since they first walked the Earth,

523
00:43:36,240 --> 00:43:41,500
it has inspired the pursuit of science and perhaps even the birth of science.

524
00:43:42,220 --> 00:43:46,620
And while life and property remain at risk of falling to its destructive forces,

525
00:43:46,621 --> 00:43:50,840
the drive to predict the weather will surely endure.

